\begin{abstract}
We study the precise sense in which collision-based factoring methods for a semiprime $N = pq$ all pay the same price, and the precise sense in which they do not. Our starting point is an exact \emph{factor-reveal} characterisation: for distinct primes $p, q$ and any integer $s$, $\gcd(s, pq) = p$ if and only if $p \mid s$ and $q \nmid s$. Specialised to $s = a+b+c$, this makes a 3SUM solution modulo a hidden prime factor into a factoring witness, connecting 3SUM and integer factoring through a single divisibility lemma that also subsumes sumset differences and Pollard's $p-1$ values. We then quantify the cost of producing such a witness. We prove an \emph{arity-uniform} pigeonhole theorem: for every arity $r$, a collision search over the $r$-subsets of a $k$-set is guaranteed to succeed against every evaluation into $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ if and only if $\binom{k}{r} > p$; sufficiency is the pigeonhole principle and necessity is an adversary construction, so the threshold $p+1$ is exactly optimal. Hence the required \emph{search-set size} improves with arity ($k \gtrsim p$, $\sqrt{2p}$, $(6p)^{1/3}$ at arities $1,2,3$) while the \emph{enumeration cost} stays above $p \ge \sqrt{N}$ at every arity. We then close two loopholes. First, an unconditional lower bound on the only interface these methods use: a query set $Q$ of nonzero integers bounded by $M$ revealing a factor of every semiprime built from a prime pool $P$ must satisfy $|P| \le |Q|\log_2 M + 1$. Second, the deterministic row is \emph{not} tight for randomised search: the exact count $\ff{p}{m} = p(p-1)\cdots(p-m+1)$ of collision-free evaluations among $p^m$, together with the integer union bound $p^{m+1} \le p\,\ff{p}{m} + \binom{m}{2}p^m$, shows that $m^2 < p$ forces a strict majority of evaluations to be collision-free; hence success probability exceeding $1/2$ requires $m \ge \sqrt{p}$, i.e.\ $N^{1/4}$ for a balanced semiprime. The resulting picture is a two-level barrier --- deterministic $\sqrt{N}$, randomised $N^{1/4}$ --- both unconditional and both independent of the arity.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} 3SUM, birthday bound, integer factoring, semiprime, pigeonhole principle, gcd queries, falling factorial, query complexity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two problems, one witness}

The \emph{3SUM problem} asks whether a set of $n$ integers contains three elements summing to zero; the conjecture that it requires $n^{2-o(1)}$ time underwrites a large body of conditional lower bounds in fine-grained complexity. \emph{Integer factoring} asks for the prime decomposition of $N$; in the semiprime case $N = pq$ its presumed hardness underlies widely deployed public-key cryptography.

These problems meet at an elementary lemma. If $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime and one has integers $a,b,c$ with
\[
a+b+c \equiv 0 \pmod p, \qquad a+b+c \not\equiv 0 \pmod q,
\]
then $\gcd(a+b+c, N) = p$ exactly. The 3SUM solution \emph{modulo the hidden factor} is a factoring witness, and extracting the factor costs one Euclidean algorithm. This is not a factoring algorithm --- one cannot search modulo $p$ without knowing $p$ --- but it is a structural bridge, and it raises a quantitative question: how expensive is it to produce such a witness, and does arity $3$ buy anything over arity $2$ or $1$?

\subsection{The hierarchy and its folklore}

Collision-based methods form a natural hierarchy graded by arity:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cllc}
\toprule
Arity & Collision type & Search set & Tuples enumerated\\
\midrule
$1$ & single evaluations coincide & $k \gtrsim p$ & $k$\\
$2$ & sumset: $a+b \equiv c+d$ & $k \gtrsim \sqrt{2p}$ & $\binom{k}{2}$\\
$3$ & 3SUM: $a+b+c \equiv 0$ & $k \gtrsim (6p)^{1/3}$ & $\binom{k}{3}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Folklore holds that the exponent in the search-set size improves ($1 \to 1/2 \to 1/3$) while the net cost stays $\Theta(\sqrt{N})$. We prove the first clause, prove the second clause \emph{for deterministic guarantees} and show it optimal there, and show it \emph{false} for randomised search, where the correct threshold is $\Theta(\sqrt{p}) = \Theta(N^{1/4})$.

\subsection{Notation}

Throughout, $p, q$ are distinct primes, $N = pq$, $\Zp := \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, and $\ff{p}{m} := p(p-1)\cdots(p-m+1)$ is the falling factorial, with $\ff{p}{0} = 1$.

\section{The factor reveal}

\begin{lemma}[One-sided divisibility reveals]\label{lem:reveal}
Let $p, q$ be primes and $s$ an integer with $p \mid s$ and $q \nmid s$. Then $\gcd(s, pq) = p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $p \mid s$ and $p \mid pq$, we have $p \mid \gcd(s,pq)$; write $\gcd(s,pq) = pt$. From $pt \mid pq$ we get $t \mid q$, so $t \in \{1, q\}$. If $t = q$ then $q \mid \gcd(s,pq) \mid s$, contradicting $q \nmid s$. Hence $t=1$.
\end{proof}

The symmetric statement, exchanging $p$ and $q$, follows by commutativity of the product.

\begin{theorem}[Complete classification]\label{thm:classification}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes and $s$ any integer. Then
\[
\gcd(s,pq) =
\begin{cases}
pq, & p \mid s \text{ and } q \mid s,\\
p, & p \mid s \text{ and } q \nmid s,\\
q, & q \mid s \text{ and } p \nmid s,\\
1, & p \nmid s \text{ and } q \nmid s.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Case 1: $p,q$ are coprime and both divide $s$, so $pq \mid s$. Cases 2 and 3 are Lemma~\ref{lem:reveal} and its mirror. Case 4: the gcd divides $pq$; were it $>1$ it would have a prime divisor $r \in \{p,q\}$ dividing $s$, contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact characterisation]\label{cor:iff}
For distinct primes $p \ne q$, $\gcd(s,pq) = p$ if and only if $p \mid s$ and $q \nmid s$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) is Lemma~\ref{lem:reveal}. ($\Rightarrow$): $p \mid s$ follows since $p$ is the gcd; if also $q \mid s$ then $pq \mid s$ and the gcd would be $pq > p$ since $q>1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[3SUM factor reveal]\label{thm:threesum}
Let $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime. If $p \mid a+b+c$ and $q \nmid a+b+c$, then $\gcd(a+b+c, N) = p$; in particular $1 < \gcd(a+b+c,N) < N$, a proper nontrivial factor.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:reveal} with $s = a+b+c$; properness from $1 < p < pq$.
\end{proof}

The same holds for a congruence stated in $\Zp$: if the reductions of $a,b,c$ sum to $0$ in $\Zp$, then $p \mid a+b+c$.

\subsection{An exhaustive verified instance: $N = 143$}

Take $N = 143 = 11 \cdot 13$ and enumerate all triples $1 \le a < b < c \le 11$. Exactly $15$ satisfy $11 \mid a+b+c$; exactly $0$ satisfy $143 \mid a+b+c$; hence all $15$ satisfy $\gcd(a+b+c,143) = 11$ and each reveals the factor. A concrete witness is $1+4+6 = 11$ with $\gcd(11,143) = 11$. The absence of mod-both triples is forced here, since the largest available sum is $9+10+11 = 30 < 143$.

\section{Reveal density}

\begin{theorem}[Witness count]\label{thm:count1}
For $p > 0$, the number of $s$ with $0 < s \le pq$ and $p \mid s$ is exactly $q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The multiples of $p$ in $(0,pq]$ are $p, 2p, \dots, qp$, and $\lfloor pq/p\rfloor = q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure count]\label{thm:count2}
For $p,q>0$, exactly one $s$ with $0 < s \le pq$ satisfies $pq \mid s$, namely $s = pq$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Reveal density]\label{thm:density}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes and $N = pq$. Then
\[
\#\{\, s : 0 < s \le N,\ \gcd(s,N) = p \,\} = q-1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:iff} the set equals $\{s : p \mid s\} \setminus \{s : pq \mid s\}$ within the period (using coprimality to identify ``$p \mid s$ and $q \mid s$'' with ``$pq \mid s$''), and the subtracted set is contained in the former. Apply Theorems~\ref{thm:count1} and~\ref{thm:count2}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Conditional on having produced a multiple of $p$ in one period, the reveal succeeds with probability exactly $(q-1)/q$. For $N = 143$ this is $12/13$; the single failure is $s = 143$.
\end{corollary}

The non-degeneracy hypothesis $q \nmid s$ is thus genuine but negligible: it fails on a $1/q$ fraction of witnesses, and when it fails one learns only $N$ itself, at the cost of a single gcd.

\section{The arity-uniform birthday bound}

\begin{definition}[Arity-$r$ collision search]
Fix a modulus $p>0$ and a finite set $S$ with $|S| = k$. An \emph{arity-$r$ collision search over $S$} enumerates the family $\binom{S}{r}$ of $r$-element subsets, evaluates each subset $A$ by a function $v : \binom{S}{r} \to \Zp$, and succeeds if $v(A) = v(B)$ for some $A \ne B$. The canonical $v$ is the subset sum modulo $p$. The search is \emph{guaranteed} if it succeeds for \emph{every} $v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sufficiency: pigeonhole]\label{thm:suff}
If $p < \binom{k}{r}$, then for the subset-sum evaluation there are distinct $r$-subsets $A \ne B$ of $S$ with $\sum_{a \in A} a \equiv \sum_{b\in B} b \pmod p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The family has $\binom{k}{r} > p$ members and the residue map lands in a set of size $p$; such a map cannot be injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity: adversary]\label{thm:nec}
If a family $T$ of subsets satisfies $|T| \le p$, there exists $v : T \to \Zp$ that is injective, hence produces no collision.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|T| \le p = |\Zp|$, so an injection $T \hookrightarrow \Zp$ exists; extend arbitrarily.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact threshold, uniform in arity]\label{thm:threshold}
For every $p>0$, every finite $S$ with $|S| = k$ and every arity $r$,
\[
\Big(\forall v\ \exists A \ne B \in \tbinom{S}{r} : v(A) = v(B)\Big)
\iff p < \binom{k}{r}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) $\binom{k}{r} > p = |\Zp|$, so no $v$ is injective. ($\Rightarrow$) Contrapositive: if $\binom{k}{r} \le p$, Theorem~\ref{thm:nec} gives an injective $v$ defeating the guarantee.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} is the technical heart of the hierarchy: the threshold of $p+1$ enumerated tuples is exactly optimal at every arity, not a lossy estimate.

\subsection{Search-set exponents}

\begin{proposition}
If $p < \binom{k}{r}$ then $p < k^r$, i.e.\ $k > p^{1/r}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
If $p < \binom{k}{2}$ then $2p < k^2$, i.e.\ $k > \sqrt{2p}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
If $p < \binom{k}{3}$ then $6p < k^3$, i.e.\ $k > (6p)^{1/3}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
One shows $6\binom{n}{3} \le n^3$ by induction, using Pascal's rule $\binom{n+1}{3} = \binom{n}{3} + \binom{n}{2}$, the bound $2\binom{n}{2} \le n^2$, and $(n+1)^3 = n^3 + 3n^2 + 3n + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Higher arity never hurts]
If $r < k/2$ and $p < \binom{k}{r}$, then $p < \binom{k}{r+1}$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{The $\sqrt{N}$ wall}

\begin{theorem}[Barrier]\label{thm:sqrtbarrier}
Let $N = pq$ with $q \le p$ and let $C > p$. Then $\lfloor \sqrt{N} \rfloor < C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$pq \le p^2$, so $\lfloor\sqrt{pq}\rfloor \le p < C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Hierarchy barrier]
For every arity $r$: a guaranteed arity-$r$ collision search over $S$ modulo $p$ enumerates $\binom{|S|}{r} > p \ge \lfloor\sqrt{N}\rfloor$ tuples for any semiprime $N = pq$ with $q \le p$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{A verified threshold table}

At $p = 100$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
Arity $r$ & Minimal $k$ & $k$ insufficient & Tuples $\binom{k}{r}$\\
\midrule
$1$ & $101$ & $100$ & $101$\\
$2$ & $15$ & $14$ & $105$\\
$3$ & $10$ & $9$ & $120$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Indeed $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ and $\binom{9}{3} = 84$ fall short while $\binom{15}{2} = 105$ and $\binom{10}{3} = 120$ suffice. The search-set sizes strictly decrease, $101 > 15 > 10$, while all tuple counts exceed $p = 100$: a tenfold compression of the search set with no reduction whatever in the work.

\section{Arity reduction and the unity of reveal mechanisms}

For finite $S \subseteq \Zp$ write $S+S := \{x+y : x,y \in S\}$, so $|S+S| \le |S|^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Arity reduction]\label{thm:arityred}
For any finite $S \subseteq \Zp$,
\[
\big(\exists\, a,b,c \in S : a+b+c = 0\big) \iff \big(\exists\, c \in S : -c \in S+S\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) $a+b = -c$ with $a,b \in S$. ($\Leftarrow$) $-c = a+b$ with $a,b,c \in S$ gives $a+b+c=0$.
\end{proof}

Algorithmically: the sumset table costs $|S|^2$ additions, after which the 3SUM search is $|S|$ membership queries. Arity $3$ \emph{is} the arity-$2$ structure interrogated $|S|$ more times.

\begin{lemma}[Fermat step]\label{lem:fermat}
Let $p$ be prime with $p \nmid a$ and $(p-1) \mid k$. Then $p \mid a^k - 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $k = (p-1)m$; in $\Zp$, $a \ne 0$ gives $a^{p-1} = 1$, so $a^k = 1$; lift back to $\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pollard $p-1$ reveal]
Let $p \ne q$ be primes, $p \nmid a$, $(p-1)\mid k$ and $q \nmid a^k-1$. Then $\gcd(a^k-1, pq) = p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Collision reveal]\label{thm:colreveal}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes and $x \ge y$ with $x \equiv y \pmod p$ and $q \nmid x-y$. Then $\gcd(x-y, pq) = p$.
\end{theorem}

Theorems~\ref{thm:threesum}, the Pollard reveal and~\ref{thm:colreveal} are three faces of Lemma~\ref{lem:reveal}: sumset differences, 3SUM sums, $a^k-1$ and singular-moduli differences all feed the same divisibility test, which is what licenses treating them as one hierarchy.

\begin{theorem}[Pipeline, at every arity]
Let $p \ne q$ be primes, $S$ a finite set of naturals and $r$ an arity with $p < \binom{|S|}{r}$. Then there are distinct $r$-subsets $A \ne B$ of $S$ whose sums differ by some $d$ with $p \mid d$; and if $q \nmid d$, then $\gcd(d, pq) = p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:suff} gives the sum collision, hence $p \mid d$; Theorem~\ref{thm:colreveal} finishes.
\end{proof}

\section{An unconditional gcd-query lower bound}

\begin{definition}[gcd-query algorithm]
A \emph{gcd-query algorithm} produces a finite set $Q$ of nonzero integers, each at most $M$, and computes $\gcd(x,N)$ for $x \in Q$; it \emph{solves} $N$ if some query has a nontrivial gcd with $N$. Define the \emph{touched set} $U(Q) := \bigcup_{x \in Q}\{\pi \text{ prime} : \pi \mid x\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:log}
Every nonzero $x$ has at most $\log_2 x$ distinct prime factors.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The product of $t$ distinct prime factors divides $x$ and is at least $2^t$, so $2^t \le x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:touched}
If $x \le M$ for all $x \in Q$, then $|U(Q)| \le |Q|\log_2 M$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:untouched}
If $p \ne q$ are primes with $p,q \notin U(Q)$, then $\gcd(x,pq) = 1$ for every nonzero $x \in Q$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Neither prime divides any query, so case 4 of Theorem~\ref{thm:classification} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[gcd-query lower bound]\label{thm:gcdquery}
Let $Q$ be a finite set of nonzero integers, each $\le M$, and $P$ a finite set of primes. If for all distinct $p,q \in P$ some $x \in Q$ has $\gcd(x,pq) \ne 1$, then
\[
|P| \le |Q| \cdot \log_2 M + 1, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad |Q| \ge \frac{|P|-1}{\log_2 M}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $|P| > |Q|\log_2 M + 1$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:touched}, $|P| > |U(Q)| + 1$, so $|P \setminus U(Q)| > 1$ and there are distinct untouched $p,q \in P$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:untouched} every query has trivial gcd with $pq$, contradicting the solving hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If all queries are below $2^{64}$, covering a pool of $n$ candidate primes requires at least $(n-1)/64$ queries.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity]
If $p \mid x$ and $q \nmid x$ for distinct primes $p,q$, then $\gcd(x,pq) = p \ne 1$: a single revealing query solves the instance.
\end{proposition}

Thus Theorem~\ref{thm:gcdquery} does not say gcd testing is expensive --- it is one Euclidean algorithm --- but that the \emph{search} for a query touching the right prime is expensive, since each query touches at most $\log_2 M$ primes. For a balanced semiprime the candidate primes near $\sqrt N$ number about $\sqrt N / \log\sqrt N$, so with $\log_2 M = O(\log N)$ one obtains
\[
|Q| \ \ge\ \Omega\!\left(\frac{\sqrt N}{\log^2 N}\right),
\]
a $\tilde\Omega(\sqrt N)$ bound for the non-adaptive worst-case guarantee, with no pigeonhole hypothesis and no assumption on how queries are generated.

\section{The counting birthday bound and the randomised barrier}

Real collision-based factoring is randomised: Pollard's rho finds a collision modulo $p$ after about $\sqrt p \approx N^{1/4}$ values. If the deterministic wall were the whole truth, rho could not work. We supply the missing row as a proved lower bound.

Model the enumeration of $m$ distinct tuples as an assignment $\{1,\dots,m\} \to \Zp$; there are $p^m$ of them, and one is \emph{collision-free} if it is injective.

\begin{theorem}[Exact count of collision-free evaluations]\label{thm:count}
The number of collision-free assignments of $m$ tuples into $\Zp$ is $\ff{p}{m} = p(p-1)\cdots(p-m+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Collision-free assignments are exactly injections of an $m$-set into a $p$-set, of which there are $\ff{p}{m}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integer union bound]\label{thm:union}
For all $p$ and all $m \le p$,
\[
p^{\,m+1} \ \le\ p \cdot \ff{p}{m} + \binom{m}{2} p^{\,m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $m$; the base case reads $p \le p$. For the step, multiply the hypothesis by $p$ to get $p^{m+2} \le p(p\,\ff{p}{m}) + \binom{m}{2}p^{m+1}$, then split $p = (p-m) + m$ and use $\ff{p}{m} \le p^m$:
\[
p\big(p\,\ff{p}{m}\big) = (p-m)\,p\,\ff{p}{m} + m\,p\,\ff{p}{m} \le p\big((p-m)\ff{p}{m}\big) + m\,p^{m+1}.
\]
Since $(p-m)\ff{p}{m} = \ff{p}{m+1}$ and $\binom{m}{2} + m = \binom{m+1}{2}$, collecting terms gives the claim.
\end{proof}

Dividing by $p^{m+1}$ recovers the familiar probabilistic form: for a uniformly random assignment of $m$ items into $p$ boxes,
\[
\Pr[\text{some collision}] = 1 - \frac{\ff{p}{m}}{p^m} \le \frac{\binom{m}{2}}{p},
\]
one term per pair. Theorem~\ref{thm:union} is this inequality cleared of denominators, so no real-number probability theory is needed.

\begin{theorem}[Majority collision-free]\label{thm:majority}
Let $0 < p$ and $m \le p$ with $2\binom{m}{2} < p$. Then $p^m < 2\,\ff{p}{m}$: strictly more than half of all assignments are collision-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $2\ff{p}{m} \le p^m$. Multiplying by $p$ and combining with Theorem~\ref{thm:union} in the form $p\cdot p^m \le p\,\ff{p}{m} + \binom{m}{2}p^m$ yields $p \le 2\binom{m}{2}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Randomised $\sqrt p$ barrier]\label{thm:randbarrier}
Let $0 < p$ and $m^2 < p$. Then $p^m < 2\,\ff{p}{m}$. Consequently any collision search enumerating fewer than $\sqrt p$ tuples fails on a strict majority of evaluations, so success probability exceeding $1/2$ requires at least $\sqrt p$ tuples.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$m^2 < p$ gives $m \le p$ and $2\binom{m}{2} = m(m-1) \le m^2 < p$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:majority}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-level barrier]
For a balanced semiprime $N = pq$ with $p \approx q \approx \sqrt N$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Regime & Tuples needed & In terms of $N$\\
\midrule
Deterministic guarantee & $> p$ & $\sqrt N$\\
Randomised, success probability $> 1/2$ & $\ge \sqrt p$ & $N^{1/4}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Both rows are lower bounds, and both are independent of the arity $r$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Numeric instance]
Take $p = 10007$ and $m = 100 \approx \sqrt p$. Then $2\binom{100}{2} = 9900 < 10007$, so $10007^{100} < 2\cdot \ff{10007}{100}$: with only $100$ tuples a strict majority of all evaluations remain collision-free, while a deterministic guarantee at the same modulus needs more than $10007$ tuples. The thresholds differ by two orders of magnitude, and the gap widens as $\sqrt p$.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Witness-to-factor extraction.} Given $N$ and a candidate witness $s$, compute $g = \gcd(s \bmod N, N)$ and return $g$ if $1 < g < N$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:classification}; cost is $O(\log^2 N)$ bit operations. By Theorem~\ref{thm:density}, a witness drawn uniformly from the multiples of $p$ in one period succeeds with probability $(q-1)/q$.

\paragraph{Arity-$r$ collision search.} Enumerate the $\binom{|S|}{r}$ subsets, keying a dictionary by the subset sum reduced modulo $N$ (one cannot reduce modulo the unknown $p$), and on a repeated key offer the difference of the two sums to the extraction routine. Theorem~\ref{thm:suff} guarantees a collision once $\binom{|S|}{r} > p$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} says nothing smaller can. Time and space are $\Theta(\binom{|S|}{r}) = \Omega(\sqrt N)$ for guaranteed success.

\paragraph{Arity reduction for 3SUM.} Build the sumset table $\{a+b\}$ as a hash map to witnessing pairs in $|S|^2$ operations, then look up $-c$ for each $c \in S$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:arityred}; time $O(|S|^2)$, space $O(|S|^2)$.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Reading the hierarchy correctly.} The arity story features a real exponent improvement --- the required set size drops from $\Theta(p)$ to $\Theta(\sqrt p)$ to $\Theta(p^{1/3})$ --- and yet the cost is unchanged. The improvement is in the \emph{generating set}; the cost lives in the \emph{tuple count}. Any claimed speedup should be asked which quantity its exponent improved.

\paragraph{A bridge for fine-grained complexity.} Theorem~\ref{thm:threesum} makes a 3SUM solution modulo a hidden prime a factoring witness, and Theorem~\ref{thm:arityred} places 3SUM exactly one layer above the sumset --- raw material for hardness transfer in both directions.

\paragraph{Query complexity as the right abstraction.} Theorem~\ref{thm:gcdquery} does not depend on collisions at all: because every method in the family consults $\gcd(\cdot,N)$, the information-theoretic cost can be charged to the queries, each touching at most $\log_2 M$ primes. This rules out a whole design space without enumerating the designs.

\paragraph{Cryptographic reading.} Both barriers are lower bounds and hence reassurances; nothing here threatens deployed parameters. The $N^{1/4}$ randomised level is exactly the classical rho threshold, and is the reason balanced semiprimes are used: an unbalanced $N = pq$ with small $p$ collapses the randomised barrier to $\sqrt p$.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The barriers concern the \emph{search} step and say nothing about algorithms exploiting structure beyond the gcd interface, such as the number field sieve. Theorem~\ref{thm:gcdquery} is non-adaptive. The randomised barrier is stated for uniform evaluations.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Arity-independence as an information-theoretic law]
For every arity $r$ and every family $F$ of $r$-subsets of a $k$-set, a search guaranteeing a sum collision modulo $p$ must satisfy $|F| > p$, and for randomised search with success probability $\varepsilon$ the requirement is $|F| \ge \sqrt{2\varepsilon p}$ --- with no dependence on $r$ in either bound. The arity merely repackages the same $|F|$ residue evaluations, so the entropy available to the searcher is $\log|F|$ however the tuples are structured; the exponent $1/r$ lives in the size of the generating set, never in the cost.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[The gcd-query barrier is tight up to logarithms]
For every $n$ there is a query set $Q$ with $|Q| = O(n \log n / \log M)$ revealing a factor of every semiprime built from the first $n$ primes below $M$; combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:gcdquery} this pins the gcd-query complexity of factoring over a prime pool of size $n$ at $\tilde\Theta(n/\log M)$. The lower bound counts touched primes, so a matching upper bound is a packing problem --- pack primes into products of size $\le M$ --- a covering-design question rather than a number-theoretic one.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[3SUM-hardness transfer]
If 3SUM over $n$ integers requires $n^{2-o(1)}$ time, then any factoring algorithm that only inspects sums of at most $3$ elements of an adaptively chosen set $S \subseteq \Zp$, together with gcds thereof, requires $p^{2/3-o(1)}$ operations; conversely a truly subquadratic 3SUM algorithm would yield a corresponding speedup for such structured searches.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

A single divisibility lemma --- $\gcd(s,pq) = p$ exactly when $p \mid s$ and $q \nmid s$ --- unifies 3SUM sums, sumset differences, Pollard $p-1$ values and singular-moduli differences into one family of factoring witnesses, and makes 3SUM modulo a hidden prime a structural neighbour of factoring. Quantifying the cost of a witness yields a matching pair of bounds valid at every arity: guaranteed success needs more than $p$ enumerated tuples, and this threshold is exactly optimal, while the arity improves only the search-set exponent. Unconditionally, a gcd-query algorithm covering a pool of $n$ primes with queries bounded by $M$ needs at least $(n-1)/\log_2 M$ queries. And by exact counting, fewer than $\sqrt p$ tuples leave a strict majority of evaluations collision-free, so the randomised threshold is $\Theta(\sqrt p) = \Theta(N^{1/4})$, strictly below the deterministic wall. The final picture is a two-level barrier --- $\sqrt N$ deterministic, $N^{1/4}$ randomised --- both unconditional and both arity-uniform.

